title: "Temporal Equivalence Principle: Lunar Laser Ranging and the Nordtvedt Effect"
abstract: "The Temporal Equivalence Principle (TEP) is a scalar-tensor theory in which proper time is a dynamical field that couples universally to all matter via a conformal metric. TEP preserves the Weak Equivalence Principle through universal conformal coupling, but predicts violation of the Strong Equivalence Principle (SEP) via compactness-dependent screening. This work tests for SEP violation using Lunar Laser Ranging data, analysing 26,207 O-C residuals from five international stations spanning 35 years (1984-2019). Analysis detects a synodic-phase-dependent modulation correlated with cos(D): the full cleaned OLS extraction gives eta = -3.17 × 10^-4 ± 9.80 × 10^-4, Bayesian MCMC gives eta = -3.16 × 10^-4 ± 6.05 × 10^-5 at 5.22 sigma, Cook's distance excision gives eta = -3.31 × 10^-4 ± 5.84 × 10^-5 at 5.67 sigma, and precision-weighted regression gives eta = -3.50 × 10^-4 ± 1.13 × 10^-4 at 3.11 sigma. DE430 residuals provide sign-consistent cross-ephemeris validation at eta = -7.03 × 10^-4 ± 2.12 × 10^-3. The observed negative sign establishes that gravitational potential screening dominates in the Earth-Moon system, providing a candidate physical explanation for the unexplained ~1 cm synodic residual documented by Müller & Nordtvedt (1998)."
